WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.159
The ledger holds 50 accounts, 1,000 cents each.

2
00:00:06.159 --> 00:00:10.649
Transfers move money between them, so the total stays 50,000.

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:12.641
A reader wants that total.

4
00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:14.962
It walks the accounts in id order.

5
00:00:14.962 --> 00:00:17.351
Lock one, read it, add it, move on.

6
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:19.530
It has passed account 3.

7
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:24.187
Now a transfer moves 300 cents from account 40 to account 3.

8
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.220
What total will the reader get?

9
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.373
It reads the rest, and gets 49,700.

10
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:36.369
It saw account 3 before the money came, and account 40 after it left.

11
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.464
Every read was locked, and no transfer broke the books.

12
00:00:40.464 --> 00:00:42.290
The sum mixes two moments.

13
00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.063
It is torn.

14
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:52.575
In one Java run of the lesson's demo, 7,241 of 9,456 sums were torn.

15
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.217
The fix: readers stop adding.

16
00:00:55.217 --> 00:00:59.728
They read one field, latest, which points to the last finished snapshot.

17
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:02.884
Reader A reads it now, and holds version 1.

18
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.610
To build the next one, the ledger takes every lock, in the id order transfers use.

19
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.559
Now no transfer is half done.

20
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:13.973
A transfer that comes now waits, for microseconds.

21
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.157
It copies all 50 balances into a new record, with the total and a version number.

22
00:01:20.157 --> 00:01:22.649
Once built, the record never changes.

23
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:27.545
Then one volatile write publishes it, and latest now points to version 2.

24
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:31.869
Then the locks go, in reverse order, and the waiting transfer runs.

25
00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.803
Reader A read latest just before that write.

26
00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:35.847
What does it see?

27
00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:38.770
All of version 1.

28
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.096
Old, but whole.

29
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.571
Reader B comes after, and sees all of version 2.

30
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.442
Old or new, never a mix.

31
00:01:45.442 --> 00:01:49.616
A snapshot never changes, and the pointer moves in one write.

32
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:57.667
Here it is in Java and Kotlin: every lock in id order, an immutable record, one volatile write.

33
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:00.096
The rule.

34
00:02:00.096 --> 00:02:06.191
Build the whole snapshot while no transfer can run, then publish it with one volatile write.

35
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.600
Check yourself.

36
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.919
A reader asks for the latest snapshot in the middle of a transfer from 40 to 3.

37
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:15.359
Can it get 49,700?

38
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:18.289
No.

39
00:02:18.289 --> 00:02:22.225
Every snapshot was copied with no transfer half done.

40
00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:26.087
It gets 50,000, maybe from a slightly older version.
